(* The package m5-display: lines of text and numbers, buttons, bars and a
   bubble level on the display of an M5Stack core, through M5Unified. Grades: time-interval-rational, in
   milliseconds.

   ShowText s and ShowNumber x write a line, the number with three decimals,
   below the previous one; ClearDisplay () clears the display and returns to
   its first line. The default implementations take a time within the
   bounds and show nothing.

   The bounds of the running times are measured on an M5Stack Gray core and
   an M5Stack CoreS3 Lite by the profile of the device (2026-10-14): the
   lower bound is the least time measured over both, rounded down, and the
   upper bound 1.5 times the greatest, rounded up. ClearDisplay took 31.354
   to 31.814 ms. ShowText and ShowNumber took 0.858 to 2.561 ms and 0.061 to
   1.597 ms, on lines of at most 14 characters; a line written below the
   display, which draws nothing, takes the least time, and ShowText, which
   writes its line as ShowNumber does, has the lower bound of ShowNumber,
   below the least time measured for it on the display. Their upper bound
   assumes a full line, of 26 characters of 12 pixels on a display 320
   pixels wide, at the greatest time per character measured, 0.32 ms (1.597
   ms for a number of at least 5 characters): 8.3 ms, 12.5 ms with the
   margin. A longer text, which wraps over several lines, is not within
   these bounds.

   The bounds hold on both boards, and the descriptor gives none per board:
   ClearDisplay took 31.354 to 31.469 ms on the Gray and 31.739 to 31.814 ms
   on the CoreS3, which give the same bounds, and those of ShowText and
   ShowNumber are those of a full line.

   ShowTextAt (x, y, size, s) writes s at the pixel (x, y) from the top left
   corner, in text of the size 1 to 4 (a larger size is 4, a smaller 1),
   over the band from x to the right edge of the display, 10 times the size
   pixels high, which it fills with the background first, so that it
   replaces the text of that band; the text is centred vertically in the
   band, and starts at x on the black background of the display and 3 times
   the size pixels right of x on a band of another colour; ShowNumberAt (x, y, size, decimals, v)
   writes v with the given number of decimals, at most 6, likewise. The next
   line of ShowText or ShowNumber starts at the left edge below the band.
   They leave the rest of the display as it is, and ClearDisplay clears it.

   ShowTimeAt (x, y, size, s, t) writes s followed by the duration of t
   seconds as minutes and seconds, m:ss, likewise. ShowColourTextAt (x, y,
   size, foreground, background, s) writes s likewise in the colour
   foreground over the band filled with the colour background, each a
   colour of 24 bits, 0xRRGGBB, and ShowColourNumberAt (x, y, size,
   decimals, foreground, background, v) writes v likewise, with the given
   number of decimals as ShowNumberAt.

   The upper bound of ShowTextAt and ShowNumberAt assumes the widest band,
   of size 4 from the left edge, 320 by 40 pixels of 16 bits at 40 MHz,
   5.1 ms, and a full line of 13 characters of size 4, at four times the
   greatest time per character measured at size 2, 1.28 ms each: 21.7 ms,
   33 ms with the margin. A text that wraps over several lines is not within
   these bounds. Their lower bound is that of ShowText. On an M5Stack CoreS3
   Lite the profile of the device (2026-10-14) measured ShowTextAt at 2.599
   to 5.498 ms and ShowNumberAt at 1.818 to 4.119 ms, on some texts of the
   digital level only; a shorter text took 2.404 ms, below these, so the
   least time depends on the text and the lower bound stays that of
   ShowText on every board. ShowTimeAt and ShowColourTextAt have the bounds
   of ShowTextAt, and ShowColourNumberAt those of ShowNumberAt, PROVISIONAL,
   not yet measured.

   DrawButton (x, y, w, h, s) draws a button: a rectangle with rounded
   corners from the pixel (x, y), w by h pixels, filled with dark grey and
   outlined in white, with the label s centred in white, in the greatest
   text size among 2, 1.5 and 1 at which it fits within the rectangle less
   4 pixels on each side. DrawBar (x, y, w, h, n, colour) draws a horizontal
   bar from the pixel (x, y), w by h pixels: its first n pixels from the
   left, at most w, filled with the colour, and the rest with dark grey.
   DrawLevel (x, y, r, dx, dy, colour) draws a bubble level: a vial, the
   circle of radius r centred at the pixel (x, y), outlined in white, with a
   circle of radius r / 4 at its centre outlined in grey, and a bubble, the
   disc of radius r / 6 filled with the colour, at the offset (dx, dy) in
   pixels from the centre, moved along it towards the centre so as to lie
   within the vial. The vial is drawn by the first call and redrawn, with
   the bubble, by a call that moves the bubble or changes its colour, the
   previous bubble being erased; a call that does neither draws nothing.

   The bounds of DrawButton, DrawBar and DrawLevel are PROVISIONAL, not yet
   measured. DrawButton and DrawBar fill at most a rectangle of the display,
   320 by 240 pixels of 16 bits at 40 MHz, 30.7 ms, and DrawButton writes a
   label of a line: at most 33 ms, as ShowTextAt. DrawLevel fills two discs
   of at most a sixth of the radius and outlines two circles, of at most
   1000 pixels each if the vial lies within the display: at most 10 ms. Their
   lower bound is 0, the time of a call of DrawLevel that draws nothing. *)

operation ShowText : string ~> unit # [0.05, 12.5]
default ShowText _ = delay 1

operation ShowNumber : float ~> unit # [0.05, 12.5]
default ShowNumber _ = delay 1

operation ClearDisplay : unit ~> unit # [31, 48]
default ClearDisplay () = delay 31

operation ShowTextAt : nat * nat * nat * string ~> unit # [0.05, 33]
default ShowTextAt _ = delay 3

operation ShowNumberAt : nat * nat * nat * nat * float ~> unit # [0.05, 33]
default ShowNumberAt _ = delay 3

operation ShowTimeAt : nat * nat * nat * string * nat ~> unit # [0.05, 33]
default ShowTimeAt _ = delay 3

operation ShowColourTextAt : nat * nat * nat * nat * nat * string ~> unit
    # [0.05, 33]
default ShowColourTextAt _ = delay 3

(* The bounds of ShowColourNumberAt are those of ShowNumberAt, not measured
   separately. *)
operation ShowColourNumberAt
    : nat * nat * nat * nat * nat * nat * float ~> unit # [0.05, 33]
default ShowColourNumberAt _ = delay 3

operation DrawButton : nat * nat * nat * nat * string ~> unit # [0, 33]
default DrawButton _ = delay 3

operation DrawBar : nat * nat * nat * nat * nat * nat ~> unit # [0, 33]
default DrawBar _ = delay 1

operation DrawLevel : nat * nat * nat * int * int * nat ~> unit # [0, 10]
default DrawLevel _ = delay 1
